          "session_id": "full_no_memory-g-md-009",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My TSH is a little high but my free T4 is still in the normal range. What does that mean, what does guidance describe about treating it, and how often should it be rechecked?",
          "answer": "You should seek care to discuss your elevated TSH levels. \n\nA high TSH with a normal free T4 level typically indicates subclinical hypothyroidism. This condition suggests that while your thyroid is producing enough hormone, the pituitary gland is signaling for more due to perceived insufficiency (consultation, guideline-hypothyroidism-testing-and-monitoring). \n\nTreatment recommendations for subclinical hypothyroidism vary significantly, particularly for TSH levels between the upper normal limit and 10 mIU/L. Some guidelines suggest initiating treatment if symptoms, positive antibodies, or cardiovascular risk factors are present, while others may recommend monitoring without treatment (consultation, research). If your TSH is persistently above 10 mIU/L, treatment is generally more strongly considered (consultation, research). \n\nAs for monitoring, follow-up TSH testing is typically recommended every three to six months after treatment changes or adjustments are made (consultation, guideline-hypothyroidism-testing-and-monitoring). If you are not starting treatment, your healthcare provider might suggest regular monitoring based on your individual circumstances (research).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
